---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { scenes, ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Interlude from '../../components/Interlude.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Questions to Bring', href: '/explore/questions-to-bring/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Questions to Bring to an Astrology Reading"
  description="The questions that make a reading genuinely useful: how to shape them, examples by life area, and the ones no astrologer should answer."
  path="/explore/questions-to-bring/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Questions to bring to a reading</h1>
        <p class="lede">A good question is most of a good reading. Here's how to shape one, dozens of examples to borrow, and the handful worth leaving at the door.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.prepareReading.src}
        srcset={scenes.prepareReading.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.prepareReading.alt}
        width={scenes.prepareReading.width}
        height={scenes.prepareReading.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What makes a question work</h2>
      <p>
        The best questions are specific, honest, and about something you can actually act on. The
        difference is usually one degree of concreteness:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>"Tell me about my career"</em> → <strong>"Why does every job start well and sour in year two?"</strong></li>
        <li><em>"What about love?"</em> → <strong>"I keep choosing unavailable people. What's that pattern about?"</strong></li>
        <li><em>"Will I be successful?"</em> → <strong>"What kind of work would actually suit me, given how I'm built?"</strong></li>
        <li><em>"Is this a good year?"</em> → <strong>"I'm deciding between staying and moving in the spring. What's the timing picture?"</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Notice that the improved versions are answerable. They describe a real situation and leave
        the decision with you, which is exactly the territory a chart can speak to.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <Interlude
    image={ocean.ripplesSunset}
    eyebrow="Before you book"
    heading="A good question is most of a good reading."
    body="Ten questions in an hour produces ten shallow answers. Two or three real ones, the sort you actually lie awake with, leave room for the conversation to go somewhere."
  />


  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>By area of life</h2>
      <h3>Self-understanding</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What are the patterns I keep repeating without meaning to?</li>
        <li>Which of my strengths am I underusing?</li>
        <li>Where does my chart suggest I'm hardest on myself?</li>
        <li>What's the difference between what I want and what I was taught to want?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Work and vocation</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What conditions does my work need: autonomy, depth, audience, structure?</li>
        <li>Is the restlessness I feel about this job, or about something bigger?</li>
        <li>What does my chart say about working for myself versus within an organization?</li>
        <li>I've been offered a change. What should I be weighing that I'm not?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Relationships</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What do I actually need in a partnership, as opposed to what I look for?</li>
        <li>Why does this particular dynamic keep recurring?</li>
        <li>How do the two of us handle conflict differently, and where does that catch?</li>
        <li>What helps each of us feel secure?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Timing and transitions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What season am I in, and roughly how long does it run?</li>
        <li>Are there windows in the next year that favor this decision?</li>
        <li>Is this a moment to push or a moment to consolidate?</li>
        <li>What's the larger arc this year belongs to?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Meaning and direction</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What is my chart pointing me toward that I keep avoiding?</li>
        <li>Where do my easy gifts and my growth edge actually meet?</li>
        <li>What would "living this chart well" look like in practice?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions to leave at the door</h2>
      <p>
        Not because they're forbidden, but because no honest astrologer can answer them and the
        attempt does damage:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Anything asking for a guaranteed outcome.</strong> "Will I get the job" has no
          reliable astrological answer. "What's the picture around this application, and what can I
          influence" does.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Medical, legal, or financial verdicts.</strong> Those belong with licensed
          professionals, and you should be referred rather than answered.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Questions about a third party's private life.</strong> Reading someone's chart to
          learn their secrets isn't insight, it's surveillance, and consent matters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Death timing.</strong> Refused as a matter of ethics across the profession.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>"Just tell me what to do."</strong> Understandable, and still the one thing a
          reading shouldn't do. The choice is the part that's yours.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Bring two or three</h2>
      <p>
        Ten questions in an hour produces ten shallow answers. Two or three real ones, the sort you
        actually lie awake with, leave room for the conversation to go somewhere. Write them down
        beforehand; the good ones often arrive while you're writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        More in <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">preparing for your reading</a> and
        <a href="/how-readings-work/">what to expect</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Got your questions?"
    body="That's the hard part done. Choose a reading and bring them along."
    buttonLabel="See the readings"
    buttonHref="/readings/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
